## Five ways to randomise your intervention

Not every intervention fits the same shape. The right design depends on your intervention's structure, budget, and the learning questions you would like to answer. Here are five approaches in common use.

```{=html}
<div class="designs">
  <div class="d"><div>
    <h3>Lottery</h3>
    <p><span class="lab">How:</span> randomly select which eligible firms receive the intervention, by a documented public or private draw.</p>
    <p><span class="lab">Best when:</span> eligibility is clear, the intervention is oversubscribed, and a single treatment is being tested.</p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="d"><div>
    <h3>Multiple treatment arms</h3>
    <p><span class="lab">How:</span> randomly assign firms to different versions of the intervention - for example, grant only vs. grant plus advisory support - with or without a comparison group receiving nothing.</p>
    <p><span class="lab">Best when:</span> you want to know which design element drives results, not just whether the intervention works at all.</p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="d"><div>
    <h3>Randomised phase-in</h3>
    <p><span class="lab">How:</span> everyone receives the intervention eventually, but in a randomly assigned order. Firms still waiting serve as the comparison group in the meantime.</p>
    <p><span class="lab">Best when:</span> budget or capacity limits mean you cannot reach all eligible firms at once, and withholding support permanently would be unacceptable.</p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="d"><div>
    <h3>Randomised intensities</h3>
    <p><span class="lab">How:</span> vary the dose of the intervention across firms - for example, 100%, 50%, or 0% of eligible employees receive training.</p>
    <p><span class="lab">Best when:</span> you suspect that knowledge or behaviour spreads between treated and untreated workers within the same firm, and you want to measure that spread.</p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="d"><div>
    <h3>Encouragement design</h3>
    <p><span class="lab">How:</span> randomise who receives extra outreach or information encouraging take-up, rather than randomising access to the intervention itself.</p>
    <p><span class="lab">Best when:</span> the intervention cannot legally be denied to anyone who qualifies - for example, an open voucher scheme - but take-up is voluntary and varies.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>
```

