## When take-up is low: encouragement design

Some interventions cannot be denied to anyone who qualifies - an open advisory service or a voucher scheme, for example. In these cases, it is not possible to assign firms to a comparison group that is formally excluded from support. The encouragement design offers an alternative: rather than randomising access, randomise who receives extra outreach, information, or a targeted invitation to use the intervention.

Firms who receive the encouragement are then compared with those who do not. Because encouraged firms are more likely to take up the intervention than non-encouraged firms, the comparison still produces an estimate of the intervention's effect - even if both groups technically had access.

The main limitation is that the estimate reflects the effect of the encouragement itself as much as the intervention. If untargeted outreach - a news story, a social media post - reaches both groups, the comparison is diluted.

::: {.caution}
Compliance is rarely perfect in either direction: some encouraged firms will never take up the intervention, and some non-encouraged firms will find it and engage anyway. The right approach is to compare the whole encouraged group with the whole non-encouraged group, regardless of what individual firms actually did. Your evaluation team will handle this in the analysis.
:::


## Did randomisation lead to comparable firms in treatment and comparison group?

Once the lottery has been conducted and the baseline survey fielded, the first analytical task is to check whether the draw actually produced two comparable groups. Even a well-designed lottery can, by chance, produce a treatment group that differs from the comparison group on firm age, size, sector, or prior performance. A balance check compares the two groups on a set of baseline characteristics and tests whether any differences are larger than chance would produce. If the groups look similar before the intervention starts, the evaluation is on solid ground. If they do not, the analysis will need to account for the imbalance. [More details on balance checks and what to do if groups are not comparable](02.05a-balance.qmd)


## What are potential challenges?

Even a well-designed RCT must survive contact with reality. These are the four most common problems - and what can be done about each.

**Attrition.** Firms drop out between the baseline and the endline - because they closed, relocated, or simply refused to participate in a survey again. If drop-out is higher in one group than the other, the two groups are no longer comparable and the impact estimate may be biased. Prevention is far easier than correction: collect multiple contact details at baseline, keep them updated during the intervention, and keep the endline survey as short as possible.

**Spillovers.** Comparison firms benefit from the intervention indirectly - a treated supplier passes on improved products, or a treated neighbour shares what they learned in a training session. When this happens, the gap between the groups narrows and the evaluation underestimates the true effect. Spillovers are most likely in concentrated sectors or geographically tight clusters. Where the risk is high, randomising at a higher level - by region or sector cohort rather than by individual firm - can contain it. If spillovers are anticipated, the RCT can be designed in a manner accounting for them.

::: {.example .case data-map="romania-sm"}
[Romania (South-Muntenia): designing for spillovers]{.tag}
South-Muntenia built spillovers into the design.
About 900 applicant SMEs are grouped into roughly 100 geographic or sector clusters. Each
cluster is randomly assigned a funding intensity of 0%, 33%, 66% or 100%, and firms within
each cluster are then randomly selected for funding up to that share. Comparing funded and
unfunded firms within a cluster measures each firm's own return; comparing clusters with
different intensities measures the spillover onto neighbours and competitors.
[Read the full example](03-examples.qmd#south-muntenia).
:::

**Low take-up.** Some firms assigned to the treatment group never use the support - they decline the grant, fail to meet the conditions, or disengage early. Low take-up dilutes the estimated effect: if only half the treatment group actually received the intervention, the impact estimate reflects a half-dose. Monitoring take-up actively during implementation - and understanding why firms do not engage - is essential for interpreting what the evaluation ultimately shows.

**Uneven implementation.** The intervention is delivered inconsistently across firms - some receive more advisory time, faster disbursements, or better quality support than others. This introduces noise into the treatment and makes the evaluation harder to interpret. Clear delivery protocols, regular monitoring, and a close relationship between the managing authority and the delivery team reduce this risk.

::: {.takeaway}
None of these problems make an RCT impossible - but all of them must be anticipated at the design stage, not discovered after the endline survey. 
:::

::: {.example .romania data-map="romania-ne"}
[North&#8209;East Romania: the intervention did not achieve full take-up]{.tag}

Of the 519 firms selected by lottery, only 393 (75.7%) went on to submit a full funding proposal, and 333 (64.2%) were eventually contracted.

Because of limited take-up, it is safer to report the intention-to-treat effect, comparing everyone as originally assigned, even though it understates the effect on the firms that actually took up the grant.
:::

## Pre-register the RCT design before the intervention begins

Before the intervention begins (but latest before data analysis begins), the RCT design must be publicly committed to on a recognised registry. This is called pre-registration, and it is one of the most important quality controls in a rigorous RCT.

Pre-registration means publishing, in advance, the primary outcome, the evaluation questions, the randomisation procedure, and the analysis plan. Once that record is time-stamped and publicly visible, it becomes impossible to claim after the fact that a different learning question or outcome was always the primary focus, or that a subgroup analysis was planned from the beginning when it was actually prompted by the results.  The evaluation team should lead this process, but the managing authority should confirm it has been done before approving the inception report.

For a managing authority, pre-registration also serves a protective function. It shields the evaluation team from pressure to adjust the analysis once results are known. And it strengthens the credibility of the findings: when a null result or an inconvenient conclusion is published, pre-registration demonstrates that it reflects reality rather than selective reporting.

